const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Thesis Editing","serviceType":"Editing service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/thesis-editing/","description":"Thesis copyediting and proofreading bounded by the IPEd guidelines, July 2025. Structural issues noted, never rewritten.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Am I allowed to have my thesis professionally edited?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Almost certainly yes, within limits — and the limits are the point.\n\nThe clearest published statement is IPEd’s guidelines for editing theses, July 2025: thesis editing “should be restricted to copyediting and proofreading” and editors “should not make corrections to the content, substance or structure of the thesis”, although they “may note issues for the student’s attention”. They also ask for written approval from your principal supervisor and for the editor to be named in the acknowledgements.\n\nWhat actually binds you is your own institution’s regulation, which overrides every external guideline and will be in the graduate handbook rather than on the public website. Read it first."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why will you not check the structure of my thesis?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because restructuring is Part C work — content, substance and structure — which the guidelines say an editor should not do.\n\nThere is a practical reason underneath: you will defend this thesis aloud. If an examiner asks why chapter four is organised as it is and an editor did the reorganising, you are answering for reasoning you did not do.\n\nWhat we do instead is what the guidelines permit: note the issue for your attention. On the sample published on this page that produced six items — among them an argument that does not follow, 900 words repeated across two chapters, and a referenced appendix that was missing. All described, all located, none repaired."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I have to tell my supervisor or declare the editing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes to both, and we ask for the first before we start.\n\nIPEd asks students to obtain written approval from the principal supervisor — an email is enough — and the editor’s permission to be named in the acknowledgements. Approval converts a private arrangement into a disclosed one and surfaces any departmental restriction while scope can still change. The acknowledgement means that if anyone asks how the thesis was produced, the answer is printed in it.\n\nA better mechanism exists if you prefer: Editors Canada’s permission form, co-signed by editor, student and supervisor, stipulating what the editor may do. We supply acknowledgement wording with every job, including the clause stating that no changes were made to content, substance or structure."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does thesis editing cost, and when should I book?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"$0.035 a word for copyediting and $0.025 for proofreading, from $240. A 62,000-word thesis copyedited is $2,170 and takes ten to twenty business days.\n\nBook when the thesis is complete and nothing is provisional, and allow our turnaround plus a working week of your own. A 62,000-word copyedit produces around 1,800 tracked changes and a notes document; reviewing that properly is several days, not an evening.\n\nEditing a working draft is usually a waste of your money, because most of what we correct will be rewritten. The exception, and the cheapest useful thing we sell a candidate, is a language pass on one chapter to establish a pattern you apply to the rest."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"English is not my first language. Will you rewrite my thesis into someone else’s voice?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and this is a risk worth naming.\n\nLanguage editing for a non-native speaker is what every guideline we have read explicitly permits: grammar, article use, prepositions, idiom, sentence construction and register are all Part D work.\n\nThe danger is the over-enthusiastic editor, who smooths your thesis into fluent academic English you could not have produced — a problem in a viva, where you must read sentences aloud and defend them. We edit toward your own voice at its clearest; a sentence needing a change outside the permitted range gets a note, not a rewrite. You also get a pattern note: your five most frequent constructions, with what we changed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"I have used an AI tool. Does that change anything?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It changes what you need to declare, and guidance here is thin.\n\nThe July 2025 IPEd guidelines say nothing about generative AI at all. So the governing rule is your institution’s, and institutional AI policy is the fastest-moving area in academic regulation: a policy you read eighteen months ago is probably not the current one.\n\nWhat we will do is tell you plainly which passages read as machine-generated and keep our own editing within the permitted level. What we will not do is disguise AI-generated text. One distinction: checking writing you produced is different from using a tool to draft — and if you have done the second, that is a conversation for your supervisor first."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Editing and translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Editing and Translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/thesis-editing/"}]}];

